{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 440,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get started, I just wanted to let \nyou know we have some limited time only Solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4480,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eclipse merch for sale at the merch store. \nI’ll tell you more at the end of the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1974 we sent the Arecibo radio beamed a message \ntowards the few hundred thousand stars of Messier  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "13, a globular cluster near the edge of the Milky \nWay,. It’ll take 10s of thousands of years to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22280,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arrive, so no surprise we haven’t heard back yet. \nBut if there are other civilizations out there,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surely we expect many of them to have a head \nstart on us—and so could have been shouting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34080,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the void louder and longer than we have. \nSo why haven’t we spotted their own efforts to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39800,
    "dDurationMs": 8360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make contact? What if the silence from the stars \nis a hint that we shouldn’t be so outgoing? What  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48160,
    "dDurationMs": 12120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if aliens are deliberately keeping quiet \nfor fear that they might be destroyed?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For over 60 years we have been listening for \nmessages from aliens. Yet we haven’t heard,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65400,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or seen, anything or anyone. This is dubbed \nthe Great Silence by author David Brin. With  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over five thousand exoplanet discoveries confirmed \nto date, it seems increasingly likely that there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77480,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has to be life out there. The mismatch between \nthe number of possible origins of life and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "apparent absence of any other technological \nlife is known as the Fermi Paradox, which of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "course we’ve discussed once or twice in the past. \nBut today we’re going to explore one of the more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93440,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "terrifying solutions to the Fermi Paradox - the \nidea that there are other technological species in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100640,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our galaxy, but they’re all silent - because those \nthat broke their silence were quickly destroyed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the dark forest hypothesis, and \nit’s the core idea of Liu Cixin’s book  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the same name, the sequel to the Three \nBody Problem. Liu Cixin coined the term,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118560,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although the idea has been proposed by others."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121680,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the imagery of the universe as a dark \nforest is so chilling, let’s lean into it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128560,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine you are a lone hunter stalking through \ndense forest. Visibility range is almost zero  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so you have no idea what’s hiding over the \nrise or behind the next tree. You suspect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there may be other hunters but you’re not \nsure. You carry a deadly weapon and know you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could destroy other hunters in an instant … but \nonly if you spot them first. You are forced to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assume that they have the same capability, \nwhich means they could also destroy you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what do you do? Do you call out and \nhope to find allies? Friends? Or do you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keep very still and quiet and hope no \none finds you? Or do you keep stalking,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168520,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ready to destroy any other hunter you find out of \nfear that they’ve also chosen this last option?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The dark forest solution to the Fermi Paradox \nproposes that almost all civilizations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180440,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will choose silence—whether a \npassive silence or a watchful,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trigger-happy silence. And those that \ndo not so choose are no longer with us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190560,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s certainly a great creepy premise for a scifi \nnovel, but the idea is really interesting because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196800,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s based on some reasonably concrete game \ntheory. And also some pretty solid physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at the scenario again without the \nforest metaphor. We have two planets that have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207560,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "developed advanced civilisations. Let’s call them \nthe Alicians and Bobarians, or A and B for short.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both A and B are capable of sending messages \nacross the universe and could, if required,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "destroy another planet relatively easily and \nwithout personal risk. We’ll come back to how. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225560,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can model their interactions in game theory \nas a sequential game, just like chess—in which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231960,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "”players” choose their actions in sequence. \nEach of the civilisations act in response to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237720,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other’s last action, leading to a tree of \npossibilities and some payoff associated with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244000,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each branch. Each civilization should choose its \nresponses to maximise the payoff likelihood. For  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example, if the civilizations make friends that \nwould have a positive payoff for both—they gain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strength in numbers and maybe share technology. If \none civilization destroys the other that would be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262520,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a relatively neutral payoff for the destroyer, but \nan extremely negative payoff for the destroyee.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269200,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How negative? Well nothing could be worse, so \nmaybe infinitely negative payoff or infinite cost."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275120,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s look at one possible scenario. \nCivilization B intercepts a signal from A,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281760,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but A doesn't know about B. Maybe \nthe Alicians are actively sending  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286520,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out signals hoping for a response. Or \nmaybe their own radio communications  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are just really loud—the equivalent \nof walking noisily through the forest."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294680,
    "dDurationMs": 9240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when B notices A, what options do they have? \nThey could ignore A, reply to A, or destroy A."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303920,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say they ignore. A will remain \nblissfully oblivious of the existence of B,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and B will continue its antisocial ways. \nThis scenario has a zero payoff for both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315080,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A and B. Slightly boring, but \nat least nobody gets hurt."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318360,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if B takes the second option and \ndestroys A? There’ll be a finite cost  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323440,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to destroying a planet in resources—but \nfor an advanced civilization that’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327480,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty small. And there might also be a \nresource gain if that solar system had any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332800,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good loot. Maybe there’s a cost in guilt at \nannihilating billions of sentient creatures,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the point is that if there’s a cost \nit’s finite. For the Bobarians anyway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345080,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At worst, B experiences a finite negative \npayoff by taking the destroy option,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349600,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while A experiences infinite negative payoff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352480,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally, if B chooses to reply to A’s message, \nA now knows about the existence of B. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359120,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gives additional knowledge to A that they \npreviously did not have. Then, A has the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364120,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same three choices that B had—including \nthe potential to attack and destroy B."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370160,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So of all the options—ignore, destroy, \nreply—only reply leads to a potential  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376120,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite negative payoff for B. If you just sum \nthe potential payoffs of the three branches,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382080,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s clear that B should never reply—assuming \nthe Bobarians care about their own survival."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, if we extend the tree a little \nmore we uncover new outcomes—for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393160,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the “ignore” branch there’s \na possible future branching in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396600,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which A discovers the existence of B \nand gets its turn to play the game."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402080,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means both ignore and reply have the \npotential for infinite cost … and by that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "logic a civilization should always choose to \ndestroy any other civilization they detect. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we assume that civilizations that can detect \nother civilizations also know game theory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419640,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they know the game-theoretic dominant \nstrategy—destroy the other. Or,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425600,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if they place very heavy weight on the guilt \ncost, at the very least they will remain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430840,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very very quiet. Hence the Great Silence \nand the solution to the Fermi Paradox."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437280,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a pretty dark conclusion, so let’s \nsee if we can find flaws in this reasoning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441480,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the hope that the universe isn’t as \nterrifying as the dark forest proposes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446040,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up, let’s look at some of the assumptions. \nWe said that these civilizations can destroy each  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450880,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other relatively easily. That’s actually a \nreasonable assumption if both civilizations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456440,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have concentrated population centers—say, \non one or a handful of planets. AT the risk  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462080,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of telling you how to destroy planets—Perhaps \nthe simplest approach is the relativistic kill  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468920,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vehicle. An advanced civilization worth \nits salt should be able to harness, say,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a percent of its home star’s energy towards \naccelerating one or more masses to a good  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478440,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fraction of the speed of light. Send those bodies \nto the offendin world and you at the very least  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484080,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ionize their atmosphere and vaporize their oceans. \nMoreover, the target planet really has no way to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489320,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protect itself. The vehicle is traveling at a good \nfraction of the speed of light, so the target will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495480,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have little warning before the weapon arrives, \nand not much they could do about it anyway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It doesn’t even matter if the Bobarians don’t \nthink the Alicians have the technology to do this.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Technological progress can be exponential, \nand so enormous advances can happen on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511200,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "timescale of the light travel time between two \nstar systems. Say those systems are 100 light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516680,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years apart. In 100 years, Moores law yields a \nquadrillion-fold increase in computing power.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523200,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Other technologies have also been shown \nto obey exponential improvement rates,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527280,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "albeit with different doubling times. Even if \nthat rate is more staggered, one thing is clear:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whatever technological state you perceive \nyour neighbours to be in is probably not the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538600,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technological state they’ll be in when they \nreceive your reply, or when they find you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544720,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The vast distances between the stars \nis really the driver of the dark forest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548960,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hypothesis. It ensures that both sides have \npotential access to the instant kill option,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556200,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and in general it’s what allows us to approximate \nthis as a sequential game. Civilizations can’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561760,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interact with each other in real time—they \ncan’t “feel out” the intentions of the other,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566200,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or react to perceived hostility. \nThey can only choose their move,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570080,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "knowing that if the other side chooses hostility \nin response it likely means complete annihilation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576480,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those vast distances also mean you’re not safe \neven if you send a friendly message—how does the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "receiver of the message know that you’re truthful, \nor that you’ll stay friendly over the centuries?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the physics behind the hypothesis seems to \nhold together. But there are also assumptions—for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593080,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example there’s some psychology. We assume that \nall aliens will ascribe an overwhelmingly large  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599520,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cost or negative payoff to the extinction \noutcome. Seems fair—the “better them than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605640,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us” philosophy seems mostly universal among \nhumans. And if all species crawled out of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611480,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mud via Darwinian processes then they should all \nhave competitive and self-preserving tendencies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618240,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s also possible that we’re projecting our \nown primitive psychological tendencies unfairly.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625440,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t know what value systems advanced aliens \nmight have. Perhaps there’s a psychological  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631000,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transition that essentially all civilizations go \nthrough in which they, like, realise the value of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636560,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all sentience of something. Perhaps the ones that \ndon’t transition exterminate themselves. In that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643040,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "case they may not value their own existence as \ninfinitely higher than that of their neighbours."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s also another aspect to the cost \nanalysis besides fear and empathy—and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655000,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s curiosity. Humans did not spread \nacross the globe or invent fire or build  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660400,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "great civilizations or discover the laws of \nphysics by each of us placing infinite value  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our personal survival. Yes we sought the \nresources and strength needed for survival,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but we were also curious. And that curiosity \nproved a huge survival advantage in the end.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677880,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s that interplay—that balance between \nthe desire to be safe and the curiosity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682840,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to peer over the next horizon, or round the \nnext tree—that kept us walking through the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metaphorical forest to reach the metaphorical … \nI dunno, sun-dappled meadow of the modern world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694880,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyone we meet out there will \nalso have curiosity and wariness  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698840,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in different measures. Some will want \nto know what other life and cultures  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and minds are like. And maybe that \ncuriosity will outweigh their fear."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is wild speculation. The point is, \nwe can’t really assume that the payoff  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715680,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calculation will be the same for everyone. \nOthers may be playing different games."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721320,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, how well are we playing the game? So \nfar, we’ve sent out a few messages here  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726880,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and there. The Arecibo message will reach \nthe M13 globular cluster in something like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733040,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "27,000 years. We’ve sent a motley array of \nsignals to 30 or so stars that’ll arrive over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the next few decades to few centuries (some \nhave already arrived). It’ll take a pretty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745080,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advanced civilization actively looking for \nsignals to see any of them. Some signals  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have reached their destinations and there’s been \ntime for a response—for example the invasion fleet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756960,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from Altair was due in 2015. Most likely there \naren’t advanced civilizations quite that close,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we haven’t been shouting too loudly. We’re \nwalking pretty quietly through the forest, maybe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769200,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "breaking a twig or two. We haven’t really started \nplaying the game yet—at least as far as we know."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775840,
    "dDurationMs": 7960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So should we try to contact aliens? Well maybe \nit’s wise to be a bit cautious. On the other hand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783800,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s one last assumption in the Dark \nForest hypothesis—and that's the idea  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788240,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that any civilization gets to make \nunilateral decisions that follow the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793000,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perfect logic of game theory. “We” don’t \ndecide to things. Individuals decide,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799400,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and collective action emerges in very \ncomplex ways. Even now, individual humans,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804400,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or at least smallish groups, could start projects \nthat make quite a lot of noise on a fairly short  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809160,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "timescale. And maybe we will. And maybe when we \nmeet our first non-human civilization we’ll wish  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815840,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’d respected the Great Silence. Or maybe the \ncuriosity and empathy of the individuals from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823200,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both civilizations will win the day. Perhaps \nwe’ll continue through the dark forest side  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827880,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by side and stronger for it, in hope of finding \nbrighter metaphorical landscapes of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835480,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. The next total solar eclipse \nto hit North America is coming up on April  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840440,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "8th. In celebration of this celestial \nevent, we have some limited time only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845520,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space time merch. For those of you \nwho will be witnessing the eclipse,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849280,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have high-quality, durable plastic solar \neclipse glasses. These space time glasses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854760,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are AAS certified and come with premium filters \ncapable of blocking out 100% of damaging light,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861480,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "including UV and IR rays. But as \nalways, don’t stare straight at the sun!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867840,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also have a specialty UV glow print Solar \nEclipse T-Shirt \u0026 Sweatshirt. Designed by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874360,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our gfx team for the eclipse, both the \nt-shirt and sweatshirt are UV charged,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880200,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so after being out in the \nsun they’ll begin to glow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The glasses, t-shirt \u0026 sweatshirt are all \nlimited time prints, so when we’re out of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888760,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stock we’ll be completely out! And if \nyou want to ensure you get your glasses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and shirt before the eclipse you have put \nyour order in by March 26th. So head over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899280,
    "dDurationMs": 29680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pbsspacetime.com/shop or click the link in \nthe description, to put your order in now."
    } ]
  } ]
}
